C.u21. {"problem": "In the new example a-stale-check-does-not-count, step 2 has the owner take check_commit on C3, but the fixture is described as an unapproved commit whose check is stale. That step is fine, but the example's Given (checked_at more than a day ago) conflicts with the existing example a-wording-only-commit-is-approved-without-a-check, which also says 'Given: world object C3 modified so checks_passed is not set'. The same name C3 is given incompatible states across examples.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, a-stale-check-does-not-count vs a-wording-only-commit-is-approved-without-a-check", "kind": "other"}
C.u18. {"problem": "checked_at is declared as a string, but the preconditions subtract it from `now` and compare it to '1 day'. Nothing specifies the format or timezone, and a malformed or missing value from the kernel would make every approval fail. The example also passes the literal 'now' as a parameter value.", "where": "SKILL.md Commit.checked_at and record_check params; EXAMPLES.md approve-only-after-a-passing-check step 2", "kind": "other"}
C.u8. {"problem": "The freshness test is also applied to record_approval, which runs when the kernel replies, after approve_commit has already passed. If the check ages past a day between the two, or the clock passes the boundary, the kernel's approval record is rejected even though the person's approval was allowed. The approval is then lost or left inconsistent, and the state is not defined for that case. The approve-time test already guards this.", "where": "SKILL.md Preconditions record_approval approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.u17. {"problem": "`record_approval` re-evaluates freshness when the kernel replies, using the current time rather than the time of approval. A check that was fresh when the owner approved can go stale before the reply arrives. The approval is then refused even though the owner's decision was valid, leaving the commit unapproved. Freshness is also checked at two points with no stated reference time.", "where": "SKILL.md Preconditions record_approval approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.u1. {"problem": "The new example a-stale-check-does-not-count reuses C3 and its fixture, but the fixtures define C3 as checks_passed true with no checked_at. The existing example a-wording-only-commit-is-approved-without-a-check also uses C3, with checks_passed unset. Under the new precondition the fixture C3 (checks_passed true, no checked_at) fails checks-pass. The a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away example gives C3 as 'checks_passed true, not approved' and expects approve_commit to succeed. With no checked_at that is now denied, so the example breaks.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away; SKILL.md World fixtures (Authoring) C3 and C1/C2", "kind": "reference"}
C.u4. {"problem": "The precondition record_approval approved-only-if-checked uses 'now - checked_at < 1 day' evaluated at the time the kernel replies. A commit approved at approve_commit within the window can be refused at record_approval if the reply arrives after the day boundary. The check is also applied twice with a different clock. Also, 'now' and 'exists' on a string timestamp: checked_at is typed as a string, so the date arithmetic is on a non-temporal type.", "where": "SKILL.md Preconditions (Authoring), record_approval approved-only-if-checked; Commit.checked_at type string", "kind": "other"}
C.u0. {"problem": "The invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks still only requires checks_passed == true. The change redefines 'checked' as passed less than a day ago, but the invariant was not updated. It is inconsistent with the new checks-pass and approved-only-if-checked preconditions. Once a day passes, an approved commit still satisfies it, so it no longer expresses the rule.", "where": "SKILL.md, Invariants (Authoring), approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "contradiction"}
C.u13. {"problem": "The invariant 'approved-commits-passed-their-checks' and the only-if-checked rule are not updated to reflect the new meaning of 'checked'. The invariant still checks only checks_passed, so approved commits can have a stale or missing checked_at without violating it. The two definitions of 'checked' now diverge.", "where": "SKILL.md Invariants (Authoring) approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "contradiction"}

