A.u8. {"problem": "The invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks still says only `checks_passed == true or wording_only`. It was not updated for freshness, so it no longer matches the rule it should mirror (approve_commit and record_approval). An approved commit with a stale or missing check passes the invariant. Its reason text 'passed its change check' also omits the one-day condition.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.u21. {"problem": "The scenario passes checked_at: now() as a param, which may not be evaluated as an expression in scenario params. If it is taken literally, the following approve step would fail the freshness check.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenario approve-only-after-a-passing-check, step 2", "kind": "other"}
A.u23. {"problem": "Existing commits that passed their check before this change have no checked_at. World fixture C3 has checks_passed: true with no checked_at, so it can no longer be approved until it is re-checked. The same holds for any deployed record. Any scenario or flow that approves such a commit without a re-check now breaks. The change does not backfill or migrate these commits.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, world fixtures C3 (and C1) vs. approve_commit checks-pass", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u15. {"problem": "The invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks and the glossary entry for 'change check' and 'approval' still describe the old rule, with no freshness condition. An approved commit's check stays stale forever, which is fine for the invariant, but the wording no longer matches the approval rule. The invariant's reason also does not mention the freshness requirement.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, invariants.approved-commits-passed-their-checks and glossary", "kind": "reference"}
A.u26. {"problem": "The new one-day limit is attributed to the old interview quote 'all the recommendations agreed' (L6 / L11). That interview predates the rule and does not support a freshness requirement. The rewritten reasons keep these sources, so the provenance is misleading. The checks-pass rule is still status 'proposed'.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, approve_commit checks-pass and record_approval approved-only-if-checked (source)", "kind": "other"}
A.u0. {"problem": "record_check now has a required checked_at param, but its only caller is the kernel's change_check reply. Nothing in the diff shows the kernel sends it. If it doesn't, record_check fails or leaves checked_at unset. Then no non-wording commit can ever pass checks-pass, and no commit can be approved. The rule also assumes now() and checked_at share a unit (seconds, given 86400), and that is not declared anywhere.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action record_check (params.checked_at) and the checks-pass / approved-only-if-checked checks", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u16. {"problem": "The reworded preconditions keep their old interview sources (L6 'agree to all the recommendations' and L11 'A for 25 and 26'). Those quotes do not support a one-day freshness limit. The change keeps the source while altering the rule's meaning. It also adds a required param to record_check, which is an interface change, yet the domain interface stays at 1 in the composition.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, approve_commit.checks-pass and record_approval.approved-only-if-checked source; toprope-self.composition.yaml, authoring interface: 1", "kind": "other"}
A.u6. {"problem": "record_check now requires a checked_at integer, but record_check is the reply to the kernel's change_check call. Nothing shows that the reply carries a timestamp. If it does not, the kernel's reply cannot be recorded, checked_at is never set, and every non-wording-only commit becomes unapprovable. The scenario passes `checked_at: now()` as a param, which also assumes params can hold an expression. The units are not stated either: 86400 assumes seconds, and checked_at is a bare integer.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action record_check (params.checked_at) and scenario approve-only-after-a-passing-check", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u13. {"problem": "Fixture C3 has checks_passed: true but no checked_at, so it is now treated as unchecked and cannot be approved. It was approvable before. C1 is also approved with no checked_at. Existing commits and fixtures with checks_passed true now behave differently, and nothing migrates or backfills them.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, world.fixtures C3 and C1", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u22. {"problem": "The kernel's change_check reply must now carry a new required `checked_at` param for record_check, but nothing in the change (the call to change_check, or any kernel-side declaration) says the kernel supplies it. If the existing kernel reply omits it, record_check fails or checked_at stays unset, and then no commit can be approved except wording-only ones. The timestamp also comes from the reply, not the evaluating clock, and its unit is unspecified: the check compares `now() - checked_at` with 86400, which only works if both are in seconds.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action record_check (params.checked_at) and check_commit's call to change_check", "kind": "other"}
A.u3. {"problem": "The scenario passes `checked_at: now()` as a param literal. It is unclear that param values are evaluated as expressions. It may be passed as the string 'now()' and fail the integer type, or be evaluated at a different time from the later precondition. The scenario also only covers the fresh case: no scenario shows an approval denied because the check is more than a day old.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenario approve-only-after-a-passing-check, step 2", "kind": "other"}
A.u17. {"problem": "record_check now requires a checked_at param, but the kernel's change_check reply that feeds it is not shown to supply one. If it does not, every check result is rejected, so no commit can pass and be approved. The kernel also reports the timestamp itself, so the freshness test trusts the reporter's clock, and the unit (seconds, given 86400) is not stated anywhere. Adding a required param is also a breaking change for the kernel caller, yet the composition still pins interface 1.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action record_check (params.checked_at); toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u4. {"problem": "The modified checks-pass and approved-only-if-checked preconditions keep their old source and status. They cite the old interview line ('agree to all recommendations', L6 and L11), which does not support the new one-day limit. The new 86400-second rule has no confirmation from the owner. The change request cites only a rationale and no source.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, approve_commit.checks-pass and record_approval.approved-only-if-checked (source/status)", "kind": "other"}
A.u20. {"problem": "The invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks, its reason, and the glossary entry for the change check still describe the old rule (check passed, with no freshness). They are not updated to match the new one-day requirement. The invariant cannot use now() without breaking every approved commit after a day, so the rule is now stated in two different strengths.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks; glossary 'change check'", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.u19. {"problem": "The same time-dependent freshness check is repeated in record_approval's approved-only-if-checked. approve_commit and record_approval are separate steps, so a check that is fresh at approval can be over a day old when the kernel records it. The kernel's record is then refused after a person has already approved and the kernel may have merged. It is also redundant with the approve_commit precondition.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action record_approval, precondition approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u10. {"problem": "The glossary entry 'change check' says a commit runs the check before approval and gives no freshness limit. The preconditions' reason text now says the check must be less than a day old.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, glossary.change check", "kind": "reference"}
A.u1. {"problem": "Commits checked before this change have checks_passed true but no checked_at, so they can no longer be approved or recorded as approved until they are re-checked. The fixture C3 (checks_passed true, no checked_at) is now unapprovable, and C1 is approved with no checked_at. The fixtures were not updated to match the new rule.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, world.fixtures C1/C3; approve_commit checks-pass", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u25. {"problem": "The invariant still says an approved commit passed its change check with no freshness condition, so it no longer matches the two rules the change tightened. It is also not backed by the new requirement. The glossary entry for 'change check' and 'approval' likewise say nothing about the one-day limit.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks; glossary change check", "kind": "reference"}
A.u2. {"problem": "The invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks still says only 'approved implies checks_passed or wording_only'. Its reason ('passed its change check') omits the new freshness requirement, so the invariant is now weaker than the two preconditions and does not reflect the rule. The glossary entry for 'change check' and the 'approval' definition also say nothing about checks expiring after a day.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, invariants.approved-commits-passed-their-checks; glossary.change check", "kind": "reference"}
A.u18. {"problem": "Existing data and behaviour: a commit with checks_passed true and no checked_at, such as the world fixture C3, can no longer be approved because exists(target.checked_at) fails. Any commit already checked before this change is silently treated as unchecked until it is re-run. Nothing in the world or scenarios covers this case or the stale (older than a day) case, and the scenarios only exercise a fresh check.", "where": "authoring world fixtures C1/C3; action approve_commit precondition checks-pass; scenarios", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u7. {"problem": "Commits already checked before this change have checks_passed true but no checked_at. They now count as unchecked and cannot be approved until they are re-checked. The world fixture C3 (checks_passed true, no checked_at) is now unapprovable, so it no longer shows a checked commit. The fixture C1 is approved without a checked_at, which the new rules would not allow for a new approval.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, world.fixtures Commit C1/C3; Commit.checked_at (optional)", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u12. {"problem": "The scenario step gives `checked_at: now()` as a param value. Scenario params are literal data, so this probably passes the string 'now()' or fails to evaluate. The test may break, or may not check freshness at all. No scenario covers a stale check (older than a day) being denied, or a commit with no checked_at.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenario approve-only-after-a-passing-check, step record_check", "kind": "other"}
A.u27. {"problem": "The scenario only covers the fresh path (checked_at: now()). Nothing tests that a stale check (older than a day) or a missing checked_at is denied by checks-pass, so the new behaviour is not covered. The use of `now()` as a literal scenario param value is also unverified.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenario approve-only-after-a-passing-check", "kind": "other"}
A.u24. {"problem": "The freshness test is evaluated twice, at approve_commit and again at record_approval. approve_commit calls the kernel, which merges and tags, and only then does record_approval run. If the check crosses the 24h boundary between the two, or the reply is delayed, record_approval is denied after the merge has already happened. The commit is then merged to main but never recorded as approved, so the two sides disagree.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, record_approval precondition approved-only-if-checked and approve_commit checks-pass", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u5. {"problem": "The freshness is evaluated twice, at approve_commit and again at record_approval, which runs after the kernel's approval. A check that is just under a day old at the first step can be over a day old at the second. The kernel has already merged and tagged by then, so the record is then refused and main is left inconsistent with the recorded state.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, record_approval.approved-only-if-checked vs approve_commit.checks-pass", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u14. {"problem": "record_approval re-evaluates the freshness precondition with now() when the kernel records the approval. Approval is requested at one time and recorded later. A check close to the 24-hour limit can pass approve_commit, and the merge and tag happen, but record_approval is then refused. The kernel's state (merged and tagged) and the declared state (approved is not set) then disagree. Freshness should be judged once, at the approval decision.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, record_approval precondition approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u9. {"problem": "record_approval re-evaluates the freshness check with now() when the kernel records the approval, after approve_commit has already passed it. A check that is just under a day old when the person approves can be over a day old when the approval is recorded. The kernel then refuses to record it, leaving the merge into main and the tag done while the commit is never marked approved.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, record_approval precondition approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u11. {"problem": "record_check now requires a checked_at integer, but the reply from the kernel's change_check call is not shown to supply one. If it does not, record_check cannot be recorded, so checks_passed and checked_at are never set. Every approval then fails the checks-pass precondition unless the commit is wording-only. The time unit is also unstated: the 86400 limit assumes now() and checked_at are in seconds, and the kernel could return milliseconds.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, record_check params.checked_at, the change_check call in check_commit, and the 86400 comparison in checks-pass", "kind": "behaviour"}
